reims-vgpu: let MRT draws batch, and say what a frame interval waits for.

On Home-screen page swipes (~10 presents/s, every
frame one CmdExecIndirect2 of 28-46 draws plus one CmdDisplaySwapMapping):
every draw of the iOS compositor carries a secondary colour attachment
(store_routes mrt_draw_multi = draws), and JoinTerms barred MRT outright
(nojoin_mrt = draws, engine_delta batch_opens=0). So every draw was its own
vkQueueSubmit + fence + ring slot, and with RING_DEPTH 8 the drain worker
blocked on ring slots: draw_phase slot_us 95-260 ms/s, ring_retire_blocks
35-55/s, while gpu_span busy_us said this device used ~25 ms of GPU a second.
The same box had another process (an Unreal game) holding the AMD GPU at
gpu_busy_percent 99 %, so each round trip waited for a GPU time slice:
readback_split fence_us/fence 6-11 ms against gpu_us/gpu ~1 ms, also in
seconds with 3-21 draws. op0x37 took 30-45 ms a frame and op0x08 11-14 ms.

The MRT rung shared its reason with the depth rung (the per-draw ad-hoc
framebuffer the deferred path used to leak). That framebuffer is cache-owned
now and both submit paths dispose through dispose_ad_hoc_attachments; the
depth rung was already relaxed for that reason, MRT was not. Secondary
barriers derive their source scope from the tracked access, a secondary
sampled later goes through the resident-sample barrier, and the pass echo
compares the framebuffer (which names every secondary view). So the default
now lets an MRT draw open and join a batch; REIMS_VGPU_BATCH_MRT=off restores
the bar (census name nojoin_mrt). With it a frame's draws and the render
store's readback (which appends to the open batch) are one submission.

Measurement, census only:
- drain_ops prints op<N>_max_us beside each opcode's sum, so a long tranche
  names its packet.
- frame_cycle (under drain_ops): presents, present-to-present interval
  (sum/max), host_us = child-packet dispatch time between two presents,
  gap_us = from the end of the tranche that carried a present (its stamp
  published, IRQ queued) to the start of the next CmdExecIndirect2 (guest
  IRQ delivery + wake + CPU work for the next frame), overlapped = next exec
  already queued behind the present, and gpu_busy = the host's
  gpu_busy_percent of every DRM card, so a GPU held by another process shows
  in the log.

Tests: exec::tests::a_secondary_attachment_alone_does_not_bar_batching,
census frame_cycle_tests (3) and drain_ops_reports_each_opcodes_longest_dispatch,
parity consecutive_mrt_draws_join_one_batch_and_keep_both_attachments (fails
with REIMS_VGPU_BATCH_MRT=off).

